`forEach` does not wait for promises. It calls your async callback once per file, and each call returns a promise right away. `forEach` throws those promises away and moves on. So the loop finishes almost instantly, `console.log('done')` runs, and the files keep processing in the background.

The `await` inside the callback only pauses that one callback. It does not pause `processAll`.

## Fixes

**Process files one at a time (in order):**

```js
async function processAll(files) {
  for (const f of files) {
    await processFile(f);
  }
  console.log('done');
}
```

`for...of` runs inside `processAll` itself, so each `await` pauses the whole function.

**Process files in parallel (usually faster):**

```js
async function processAll(files) {
  await Promise.all(files.map((f) => processFile(f)));
  console.log('done');
}
```

`map` collects the promises instead of discarding them. `Promise.all` then waits until all of them have finished.

## Which one to pick

- Use `for...of` when order matters, or when each file depends on the one before it.
- Use `Promise.all` when the files are independent and you want speed.
- If you want every file attempted even when some fail, use `Promise.allSettled`. `Promise.all` rejects as soon as the first file fails.

The same problem happens with any array method that ignores return values, such as `forEach`, `filter`, and `some`, when you pass them an async callback.
